{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "MCP App views: one HTML file per view",
  "description": "How a view for an MCP App is built and served here: a Vite pipeline that folds a folder of HTML, CSS and TypeScript into one self-contained file, the to-do form that goes through it, and the tools and `ui://` resource that hand it to a host and save what the user adds. About 12 minutes including the build. Run all commands from the repository root.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Demo: one file, and no second request",
      "description": "Run `npm run build:views`, then open `mcp-apps/dist/todo-form.html` — in a browser for the form, in the editor for the source.\n\nExpect a styled form in the browser, and in the source a `<style>` block and the whole script inline. Search the file for `src=` and for `href=`: there is not one of either.\n\n**Key point:** that is not tidiness. An MCP host serves a view from a `ui://` resource into a sandboxed iframe with a default-deny CSP — the document arrives as a string, from no origin the page can fetch from again. A `<link>` to a stylesheet, a chunk the entry imports, a font from Google: each one is a request that will never be made, and the view simply arrives broken.\n\n**Question:** the folder next door has three files. So what turns three into one, and why is that harder than pointing a bundler at them?"
    },
    {
      "file": "package.json",
      "line": 71,
      "title": "The two devDependencies and the three scripts",
      "description": "`vite` and `vite-plugin-singlefile`, both dev only: the bundle ships, the bundler does not.\n\nThe plugin is the trick itself. Vite builds a view the normal way, emitting JavaScript and CSS beside the HTML, and the plugin folds every one of those emitted assets back into the document before it is written.\n\n**Scroll up to `scripts`:** `build:views` runs the build script, and `predev` and `prebuild` call it, so `npm run dev` and `npm run build` cannot start on a stale bundle.\n\n**Key point:** `next dev` does not watch `mcp-apps/`. Edit a view while the server is running and you rebuild by hand."
    },
    {
      "file": "scripts/build-views.mjs",
      "line": 40,
      "title": "Vite as a library, not as a config file",
      "description": "The script calls Vite's JavaScript API, and `configFile: false` is the interesting half of that decision.\n\n**Key point:** a `vite.config.*` at the repository root would not belong to Vite alone. Vitest reads the same file, and this project already configures Vitest in `vitest.config.mts` — a root config written for the views would quietly reach the test runner too. Passing the options here keeps the two apart.\n\n`root` on the next line points the build at the view's own folder, so `index.html` and everything it names resolve relative to it. `viteSingleFile()` below it is the plugin from the previous step."
    },
    {
      "file": "scripts/build-views.mjs",
      "line": 49,
      "title": "One build per view, and why not one build",
      "description": "The loop runs a complete Vite build per view folder. Rollup takes several inputs quite happily, so this looks like the wasteful way round. It is deliberate.\n\n**Key point:** with two entries in one build, Rollup factors shared code into a chunk and both pages `import` it. An import is a second request, and the sandbox will not make one. The single-file property survives only while each page is its own build.\n\n`emptyOutDir: false` follows from that: the builds share `mcp-apps/dist/`, so none of them may wipe what the previous one produced. The directory is cleared exactly once, up at line 34.\n\n**Show:** the rename below the build. Vite names the entry after its source file, so every view arrives as `index.html` and only then gets the view's own name."
    },
    {
      "file": "mcp-apps/todo-form/index.html",
      "line": 36,
      "title": "The entry: index.html",
      "description": "This is what Vite follows. A `.ts` file straight in a `<script type=\"module\">` — Vite transpiles the entry it finds in the HTML, so a view is TypeScript with no separate compile step.\n\n**Show:** this line and the `./style.css` link at the top of the file. Two ordinary relative paths that a browser would fetch, and after the build neither of them exists.\n\nThe markup is a form and nothing else: a labelled input with its error line underneath, an `Add` button, an `aria-live` status line, and an empty list under \"Open to-dos\". The ids are the only contract between this file and the next two."
    },
    {
      "file": "mcp-apps/todo-form/style.css",
      "line": 16,
      "title": "Tokens, copied rather than imported",
      "description": "These are the semantic role tokens from `app/globals.css`, copied by hand — each neutral one wrapped in the host's matching style variable, with the house color as the fallback.\n\n**Distinction:** the app's pages and this view share a design system but not a document. The iframe inherits nothing — not Tailwind, not `globals.css`, not even the Source Sans that `next/font` loads, since an external font is another request the CSP refuses. Copying is what the sandbox costs, and the two blocks now have to be kept in step deliberately.\n\n**Show:** `var(--color-background-primary, #ffffff)`. When a host sends its style variables, `view.ts` sets them on `<html>` and the panel takes the host's palette; opened anywhere else, the fallback is the house look. Signal blue and the graphite button stay the brand's own.\n\nBelow this, dark is defined twice: once under `prefers-color-scheme`, once under a `data-theme` attribute on `<html>`, which `view.ts` sets from the host's theme so it wins over the OS in both directions."
    },
    {
      "file": "mcp-apps/todo-form/view.ts",
      "line": 148,
      "title": "view.ts: plain DOM, then the ext-apps App",
      "description": "The top of the file is DOM only: typed `getElementById` lookups, `Add` disabled while the trimmed title is empty, Enter submitting, and the `setTitle`/`setTitleError`/`setStatus`/`renderOpenTodos` surface the rest of the file drives.\n\nHere the MCP half begins. `App` from `@modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps` is the view's end of the protocol, talking to the host over `postMessage`.\n\n**Key point:** both handlers are assigned *before* `connect()`. The host may send the tool input and a context change right after the handshake, and a handler assigned later simply misses them. `ontoolinput` puts the model's drafted `title` into the field; `ontoolresult` renders the open to-dos that `open_todo_form` returned; `onhostcontextchanged` applies theme, style variables and fonts, and runs again on every later change. After the handshake, the context it returned is applied once too, since that is the complete one.\n\n**Show:** `onSubmit` above. Add goes back through the host with `app.callServerTool` to `submit_todo_form`, never to `add_todo`: the model drafted the title, the user commits it. A rejected title comes back as `isError` and lands under the field; a saved one renders the refreshed open list, and `updateModelContext` tells the model what was added, so its next turn knows without calling `list_todos` again."
    },
    {
      "file": "lib/mcp-app-views.ts",
      "line": 15,
      "title": "The server's side of the same file",
      "description": "`readView` is all the server needs in order to hand a view to a client: one `readFile` of `mcp-apps/dist/<name>.html`.\n\n**Show:** the two guards. The name is matched against `[a-z0-9-]+` before it reaches the filesystem, because it will come from code that names a view rather than from a constant. And a missing file does not surface as `ENOENT` — it names `npm run build:views`, which is the actual fix on a fresh checkout.\n\n`server-only` on the first line is the marker `lib/db.ts` and `lib/auth.ts` carry too: `node:fs` must never follow an import into the browser bundle.\n\n**Key point:** the `ui://` resource in `lib/mcp-server.ts`, next, is the one caller — on every `resources/read`, so a rebuilt view is served without a restart."
    },
    {
      "file": "lib/mcp-server.ts",
      "line": 61,
      "title": "The tools and the resource that serve it",
      "description": "Two helpers from `@modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps/server` instead of the base SDK's `registerTool` and `registerResource`.\n\n`registerAppTool` registers `open_todo_form` with `_meta.ui.resourceUri` pointing at `ui://ai-tutor/todo-form.html` (and mirrors it into the legacy key older hosts read). A host that sees that link fetches the resource and renders it in an iframe beside the tool call. `registerAppResource` below serves that resource from `readView`, with the MCP App MIME type `text/html;profile=mcp-app` as the default.\n\n**Distinction:** the tool's text lives in the contract as `mcpAppTools`, *not* in `mcpTools`. `ai-tutor mcp --stdio` registers everything in `mcpTools`, and a stdio server has no view to render.\n\n`open_todo_form` returns the title — the fallback for hosts that cannot show a view, since the view reads the title from the tool *input* — and the open to-dos the form lists.\n\n**Key point:** `submit_todo_form` below carries `_meta.ui.visibility: [\"app\"]`. The host leaves it out of the model's tool list and accepts it only from this server's view, so a to-do enters the list through the form only when the user presses Add. Its input schema is the contract's `CreateTodoRequest`, so a blank title is an `isError` result before the handler runs."
    },
    {
      "file": ".gitignore",
      "line": 61,
      "title": "The bundle is output, not source",
      "description": "`mcp-apps/dist/` is generated, so it stays out of the repository while the folder of sources beside it stays in.\n\n**Key point:** this one line does a second job. `biome.json` turns on `vcs.useIgnoreFile`, so Biome reads this file as well — and a bundle with an inlined script and an inlined stylesheet is exactly the artefact a formatter would rewrite on every build. Ignoring it in git ignores it everywhere.\n\nThe consequence for a fresh clone is that `mcp-apps/dist/` does not exist until someone builds. That is why `predev` and `prebuild` exist, and why `readView` phrases its error the way it does."
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/unit/mcp-app-views.test.ts",
      "line": 26,
      "title": "Contract: what the tests pin",
      "description": "Three tests, no Vite and no bundle in sight: a built file reads back, a missing one names the build command, and a name that is not a view folder is refused before any filesystem call.\n\n**Key point:** the second parameter of `readView` is what makes that possible. The test points it at a temp directory, so `npm test` passes on a checkout that has never run `npm run build:views` — the build itself is covered by running it, not by a test.\n\n**Transition:** everything up to here is plain web code. One test pins the MCP half."
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/unit/mcp-server.test.ts",
      "line": 57,
      "title": "Contract: a real client sees the link, and the form saves",
      "description": "An MCP `Client` talks to `createTodoMcpServer` over an in-memory transport pair: `tools/list` shows `_meta.ui.resourceUri` on `open_todo_form`, and `resources/read` on that URI returns the view with the MCP App MIME type.\n\nThe third parameter of `createTodoMcpServer` is passed straight to `readView`, so this test, like the previous one, reads a temp file and passes on a checkout that has never built the views.\n\nFurther down, the same client pins `submit_todo_form`: `tools/list` marks it app-only, it saves on the token's user's list and nobody else's, and it refuses an empty title without saving."
    }
  ]
}
